WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:05.360
Speaker 1: We have been seeing mass shooting after&nbsp;
mass shooting after mass shooting made national&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:10.720
headlines recently. And we have polling&nbsp;
that suggests to us, hey, you know what,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:17.440
Americans really are hungry for some gun safety&nbsp;
regulations. They want them. And there is a very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:23.120
interesting a poll from Pew Research compiled&nbsp;
by Vox, and we're going to put a Vox graphic up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:30.000
on the screen. And when you look nationally,&nbsp;
yes, led by Democrats, but Republicans also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:36.080
we see greater than 85% support for some mental&nbsp;
health restrictions on who can own firearms. We&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:40.240
see more than 80% support for background checks,&nbsp;
what we would call universal background checks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:45.040
meaning there is no situation where a gun is sold&nbsp;
that would be exempt from a background check. We&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:50.320
see about 78% of the country against open carry&nbsp;
without a permit. There are some states where you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:55.920
can open carry with no permit. And even the less&nbsp;
popular stuff like banning assault style weapons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.920 --> 00:01:02.160
or banning high capacity magazines, even those&nbsp;
provisions have close to two thirds support,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:08.320
right around 65%. So very clearly Republicans&nbsp;
have seen the polling. Republicans are now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:14.400
understanding, hey, a lot of these things are&nbsp;
popular with Americans. We as Republicans can't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:19.360
just be the party of saying do nothing,&nbsp;
arm teachers and eliminate doors or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:24.560
whatever they've been talking about. So now Mitch&nbsp;
McConnell and some other Republicans are saying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:30.960
you know, we are we are okay. We're supportive&nbsp;
of the framework that's been established for this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:36.640
Senate gun safety deal. So we're going to look at&nbsp;
a clip. But I have to tell you, I am so skeptical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:41.712
of everything that's going on here on both sides.&nbsp;
And I'll explain all of that in a moment. But here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.712 --> 00:01:47.280
is Mitch McConnell yesterday making headlines.&nbsp;
Wow. Republicans being reasonable. They support&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:51.360
that way. Do they support the bill or&nbsp;
they do they support the framework? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:56.400
that's a key distinction. Take a listen to this.
Speaker 2: Senator Cornyn, who is, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.400 --> 00:02:05.200
asked to be the point person on our side to see&nbsp;
if we can come to an outcome after these horrible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:13.520
school shootings. And his teammates indicated,&nbsp;
as you have reported, a coming together kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:22.800
of behind a framework which hopefully can be&nbsp;
turned into legislative language and passed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:30.560
for myself. I'm comfortable with the floor and&nbsp;
work. And if the legislation ends up reflecting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:42.480
what the framework indicates, I'll be supportive.
Speaker 1: Right. So a number of ifs in there.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:50.800
Right. I'm supportive of the framework which&nbsp;
hopefully will get us to a bill. And if the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:57.120
legislation reflects what's in the framework, then&nbsp;
it is hopefully something that we can support.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.680 --> 00:03:04.560
Now, there's two sides to this. This is a great&nbsp;
opportunity for Republicans to say this and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:09.120
oppose it either because while the legislation&nbsp;
ended up being different than the framework&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:15.040
or we're disappointed in what Democrats ultimately&nbsp;
put here. But there's another aspect to this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:23.520
which is the bill itself is pretty damn crappy.&nbsp;
Now, I don't want to just listen. I am not I am&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:29.040
okay with incremental change and taking steps in&nbsp;
the right direction. And if this is all that can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:32.400
be passed, well, we've got to pass it. I'm&nbsp;
going to tell you what's in this framework,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.960 --> 00:03:41.200
but it is pretty damn weak. Very, very weak. There&nbsp;
are a few different things here. So first of all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:48.720
there are red flag provisions that are in the&nbsp;
framework, meaning that you have a process&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:56.560
that will allow you to do sort of some kind of&nbsp;
for those individuals who are believed to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.560 --> 00:04:01.360
mental health conditions that should prevent them&nbsp;
from having a firearm. This is a framework that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.360 --> 00:04:06.400
would allow some of those to be established.&nbsp;
There are 19 states in Washington, D.C. that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.400 --> 00:04:10.800
already have some version of that, and that's&nbsp;
something that would be in the bill. And listen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:14.640
Republicans have been talking about this is a&nbsp;
mental health issue. So that's something they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:19.120
generally supportive of, although some Republicans&nbsp;
are saying you're kind of eliminating due process&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:23.920
instead of actually saying, well, you are now a&nbsp;
felon and you can't have a firearm, which would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.920 --> 00:04:29.440
be due process. Some Republicans are against these&nbsp;
red flag laws saying you're basically just saying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.440 --> 00:04:34.240
some psychologists can sign off and then you&nbsp;
don't you don't get your constitutional right to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.240 --> 00:04:39.760
a firearm. So that could be a problem. They want&nbsp;
a more rigorous process for background checks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.760 --> 00:04:46.160
specifically on people between 18 and 21, which&nbsp;
would include an enhanced review where you contact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.160 --> 00:04:50.320
state and local law enforcement if you have&nbsp;
criminal records that could be disqualifying.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:58.400
There's a proposal about who should have to&nbsp;
register as a firearms dealer for the purposes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.400 --> 00:05:04.480
of conducting background checks. There's some&nbsp;
squishiness to that where you can be a dealer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.480 --> 00:05:08.960
You and I would look at it and we would say, this&nbsp;
is a dealer, but they don't currently have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.960 --> 00:05:13.440
register as such and thus don't have to comply&nbsp;
with background checks laws. There should be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.440 --> 00:05:17.760
no question of that. Every sale should require&nbsp;
a background check no matter where it happens.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.760 --> 00:05:23.280
They also want new penalties penalties on straw&nbsp;
purchasing of firearms to improve the prosecution&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.280 --> 00:05:28.240
of traffickers. This is where someone who can&nbsp;
legally get guns buys a bunch of them and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.240 --> 00:05:32.800
sells them to people who cannot legally get guns.&nbsp;
Those are called straw purchases, and then they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.800 --> 00:05:38.080
want money for mental health services as well.&nbsp;
So there's nothing remotely controversial here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.080 --> 00:05:43.440
in the framework. Any sane country would do all of&nbsp;
these things and much more. But that's exactly the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:50.080
problem. Number one, the bill is so milquetoast&nbsp;
that it doesn't do almost any of the things that I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.080 --> 00:05:55.760
outlined in my framework for what should be done.&nbsp;
And at the same time, Republicans still might not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.760 --> 00:06:03.600
support it. So this is yet again, it's a good&nbsp;
chance maybe to show that Republicans will block&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.600 --> 00:06:10.720
even the weakest gun safety provisions, but at&nbsp;
the same time, maybe Republicans will support it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.720 --> 00:06:16.000
because it does so little. And then they can&nbsp;
say, look, the Democrats put together a bill,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:20.480
we supported it, and we still have gun violence,&nbsp;
which, of course, we would have because this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.480 --> 00:06:26.960
bill has absolutely no teeth. So it I hate to&nbsp;
say it, it all seems like it's a great setup&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.960 --> 00:06:33.520
for the Republican Party. Here's a bonus clip.&nbsp;
Here's Mitch McConnell being asked why people who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.520 --> 00:06:39.200
can't legally buy cigarets should be able to buy&nbsp;
semi-automatic weapons. Mitch McConnell supported&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.200 --> 00:06:44.240
raising the age at which you can buy tobacco from&nbsp;
18 to 21. And Mitch McConnell does not answer.&nbsp;

00:06:44.240 --> 00:06:46.000
Speaker 3: Why should.
Speaker 4: Americans who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:50.720
are 18 years old should not be allowed&nbsp;
to buy cigarets, which is something.&nbsp;

00:06:50.720 --> 00:06:56.560
Speaker 3: That they should be allowed to buy.
Speaker 2:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.560 --> 00:07:02.000
Good try. I've already told you my view.
Speaker 1: It's not really a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:05.680
try. It's a good question.
Speaker 2: Framework. If it leads&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.120 --> 00:07:13.680
to a piece of legislation, I intend to support&nbsp;
it. I think it's progress for the country,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.640 --> 00:07:23.040
and I think that the bipartisan group has done&nbsp;
the best they can to get total support and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.040 --> 00:07:28.480
background check enhancement for that age group.&nbsp;
I think it's a step in the right direction.&nbsp;

00:07:28.480 --> 00:07:35.120
Speaker 1: Yeah, it's so weak. A background check&nbsp;
enhancement just for those 18 to 21. So Mitch, not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.120 --> 00:07:39.920
falling for any trick questions like why should&nbsp;
you be able to get an AR 15 but not Cigarets?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.920 --> 00:07:43.680
Which is really not a trick question. It's a&nbsp;
good question. Let me know what you think is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.680 --> 00:07:48.240
the fate of this bill. Find me on Twitter at David&nbsp;
Pakman. We'll take a quick break and be right back

